C = [
("h1", "9. Order in the Gathering"),
("p", "Claim: 1 Corinthians 14:26–40 gives the order of the meeting, and it gives three grounds for it: that all be done to edifying, that God is not the author of confusion, and that Paul’s writing is the commandments of the Lord. The passage limits those who speak by number and by turn, and it does not state a form of meeting beyond that."),
("sub", "9.1 The meeting as Paul describes it"),
("p", "Paul describes what happens before he orders it. “How is it then, brethren? when ye come together, every one of you hath a psalm, hath a doctrine, hath a tongue, hath a revelation, hath an interpretation” (1 Corinthians 14:26). The picture is of a meeting in which many take part. The sentence that follows is the ground of all the rest: “Let all things be done unto edifying” (1 Corinthians 14:26). The limits that follow are limits for the sake of edifying."),
("sub", "9.2 The limits"),
("p", "Three kinds of speaker are limited, each by number. For tongues: “If any man speak in an unknown tongue, let it be by two, or at the most by three, and that by course; and let one interpret” (1 Corinthians 14:27). If there is no interpreter: “let him keep silence in the church; and let him speak to himself, and to God” (1 Corinthians 14:28). For prophets: “Let the prophets speak two or three, and let the other judge” (1 Corinthians 14:29). For a revelation to one who sits by: “let the first hold his peace” (1 Corinthians 14:30). The reason is given at once: “For ye may all prophesy one by one, that all may learn, and all may be comforted” (1 Corinthians 14:31). The speakers are limited so that each may speak and all may learn."),
("p", "The next sentence is a statement about the prophets themselves: “And the spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets” (1 Corinthians 14:32). A speaker is not carried beyond his own control. The volume reads the sentence as the ground of the limits on number and turn. It does not say more about what the sentence means for the gift, and it does not take up the questions about the prayer passages, which Study 81 reads. It also does not say what the passage means for the gifts today, and gives no date for the end of the gifts Paul regulates here, for the reason given in chapter 5."),
("sub", "9.3 “God is not the author of confusion”"),
("p", "“For God is not the author of confusion, but of peace, as in all churches of the saints” (1 Corinthians 14:33). The verse gives the second ground, and it is the broadest. The reason for order is the character of God. The last clause, “as in all churches of the saints,” says that the order is the order of all the churches and is not Corinth’s alone. The clause is read by some with the verse before it and by others with the verse after. The volume reads it as the KJV has it, with the sentence on the character of God, and notes that the verse that follows (14:34) begins a new instruction and is read in chapter 11."),
("sub", "9.4 “the commandments of the Lord”"),
("p", "Paul closes the passage with a claim: “If any man think himself to be a prophet, or spiritual, let him acknowledge that the things that I write unto you are the commandments of the Lord” (1 Corinthians 14:37). The verse is Paul’s own statement of the kind of instruction he has been giving. He does not say that every sentence of the chapter is a commandment. He says that what he writes is the commandments of the Lord. And he adds: “But if any man be ignorant, let him be ignorant” (1 Corinthians 14:38). The last two verses of the chapter sum up the order: “Wherefore, brethren, covet to prophesy, and forbid not to speak with tongues. Let all things be done decently and in order” (1 Corinthians 14:39, 40)."),
("sub", "9.5 The limits of what the chapter says"),
("p", "The passage does not say how the meeting is led, who chooses who speaks, or how long it lasts. It does not give a form. It gives limits on number and turn, a reason of edifying, a reason in the character of God and a statement of authority. N. T. Wright states the point in one clause, and the volume agrees with it as a reading of the chapter: Paul’s “central concern in 1 Corinthians 14 is for order and decency in the church’s worship.” That is the setting the chapter itself names, “when ye come together.” The volume reads the chapter as an order for such a meeting and builds no form beyond what the text gives."),
("ctbl", "The grounds in 1 Corinthians 14:26–40.", [
    ("Verse", "The instruction", "The ground stated"),
    ("14:26", "Let all things be done unto edifying", "That the church be edified"),
    ("14:27, 28", "Tongues by two or three, by course, with an interpreter, or silence", "Edifying (14:26); no interpreter, no edification"),
    ("14:29–31", "Prophets two or three, the other judge; the first hold his peace", "That all may learn and all be comforted"),
    ("14:32", "The spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets", "The speaker keeps control of himself"),
    ("14:33", "Peace, not confusion", "The character of God; the order of all churches of the saints"),
    ("14:37, 38", "What I write is the commandments of the Lord", "Paul’s own statement of authority"),
    ("14:40", "Decently and in order", "A summary of the whole"),
  ], [0.14, 0.44, 0.42]),
("sub", "9.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "The order of the meeting is given to the Corinthian church as it comes together, with many taking part.",
    "Speakers are limited by number and by turn, so that all may learn and be comforted.",
    "Three grounds are stated: edifying, the character of God, and the commandments of the Lord.",
    "The passage gives no form for the meeting beyond those limits.",
  ]),
]
